  "result": "Some mornings begin with a dramatic sunrise, but mine usually arrive quietly, through the blue glow of a phone screen and the soft complaint of the kettle. I used to think this made them unremarkable. Now I wonder whether ordinary is simply another name for unnoticed abundance.\n\nA clean cup waits in the cupboard. Water becomes steam. A neighbor\u2019s footsteps pass briefly overhead. Somewhere, a bus exhales at the curb, carrying strangers toward errands they may remember fondly years from now. None of these events asks for applause, yet together they form a small architecture of being alive.\n\nPerhaps attention is less like a spotlight and more like a window wiped clean. The world does not suddenly become more beautiful; we simply notice what was already offering itself: the orange peel curling beside the sink, a sparrow balancing on a wire, sunlight moving across a wall like a patient thought.\n\nThere is comfort in this modest scale. Grand ambitions can inspire us, but small rituals keep us company. They remind us that a life is not only measured by destinations. It is also made of pauses, repetitions, repairs, and chances to begin again.\n\nSo tomorrow morning, when the kettle clicks and the window gathers light, I will try not to rush past the scene. I will stand there for one extra breath, grateful for the ordinary miracle of having somewhere to stand, something warm to drink, and another day still willing to become whatever I make of it today.",
